          correct: 0,
          explain: 'Option A correctly identifies the logical chain and the state of evidence. The photolysis argument is straightforward and well-established: UV radiation at wavelengths <160 nm dissociates CH₄ into CH₃ radicals and H atoms, which then react to form C₂H₂, C₂H₄, C₂H₆ and ultimately tholins — none of which convert back to CH₄ efficiently in the cold upper atmosphere. At the observed methane mixing ratio (~5% in the troposphere), the photolysis timescale is ~20–30 million years, orders of magnitude shorter than Titan\'s age. Three classes of geological source have been seriously proposed: (1) Cryovolcanism — eruption of methane-rich ices or fluids from a subsurface clathrate reservoir heated by radiogenic or tidal energy; candidate sites such as Sotra Patera (a possible cryovolcanic edifice imaged by Cassini RADAR) have been identified, but no dynamically active cryovolcanism has been confirmed; the morphological evidence is ambiguous because viscous relaxation of icy terrain can mimic cryovolcanic features. (2) Clathrate hydrate destabilisation — methane trapped in cage-like ice crystal structures (Type I clathrates) could be released if tidal warming or impact events destabilise the ice crust; this model requires a large methane reservoir in the crust that has persisted since accretion, which is plausible but unconfirmed. (3) Biological methanogenesis — methanogens in a subsurface liquid water ocean could produce methane from CO₂ + 4H₂; Cassini detected slight tidal flexing suggesting a subsurface ocean, but no direct evidence of biological activity was obtained. Option B is incorrect: while Fischer-Tropsch synthesis does occur in some terrestrial settings, Cassini did not confirm it on Titan; CO is present in trace amounts (~50 ppm) but the CIRS mantle temperature profiles cannot resolve individual chemical reactions, and the 2010 claim cited does not exist in this form. Option C is incorrect: tholins are complex refractory polymers and do not undergo significant catalytic back-reaction to methane under Titan surface conditions; no such recycling cycle has been measured or proposed in peer-reviewed literature. Option D is incorrect: Saturn\'s rings do rain material onto Titan\'s ionosphere (detected by Cassini as an influx of water-group ions), but the mass flux and methane content are far too small to replenish Titan\'s atmospheric methane budget; the ring-infall source has never been proposed as a solution to the methane mystery in the scientific literature.',
