          explain: 'Option A correctly describes all three components of the argument. The serpentinisation connection: the H₂ concentration in Enceladus\'s plumes is not merely a curiosity but carries thermodynamic information. Serpentinisation — the exothermic hydration of iron- and magnesium-rich silicate minerals (Fe₂SiO₄ + Mg₂SiO₄ + 3H₂O → Mg₃Si₂O₅(OH)₄ + Fe₃O₄ + H₂; or more simply: olivine + water → serpentinite + magnetite + H₂) — proceeds rapidly at temperatures above ~90–150 °C and much more slowly at lower temperatures. The equilibrium concentration of H₂ produced by serpentinisation at the temperature and pH conditions inferred for Enceladus\'s ocean (alkaline, ~11 pH, based on silica nanoparticle constraints) is consistent with the ~0.9% volume measured by Cassini INMS — but only if the system is actively producing H₂ at hydrothermal temperatures; at lower temperatures, H₂ would be consumed by back-reactions or would equilibrate to much lower concentrations. The methanogen connection: on Earth, the Lost City hydrothermal field on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge operates by exactly this chemistry. Serpentinisation produces H₂-rich, alkaline (pH ~9–11), warm (~40–90 °C) vent fluids. Methanogens (archaea, primarily Methanosarcinales) use H₂ as an electron donor to reduce CO₂ to CH₄: CO₂ + 4H₂ → CH₄ + 2H₂O. The Gibbs free energy change (ΔG) for this reaction at Enceladus ocean conditions — calculated from the observed CO₂ and H₂ concentrations and modelled temperature — is approximately −45 kJ/mol (exergonic), sufficient to support microbial metabolism. This is not a marginal or speculative calculation; Waite et al. (2017) performed the thermodynamic analysis directly and confirmed the reaction is energetically favourable. The CH₄ excess: the most provocative finding of Waite et al. (2017) was that the CH₄ concentration in the plumes exceeds what abiotic serpentinisation + known geochemical reactions alone can produce at the measured H₂ and CO₂ concentrations and modelled temperatures. While the authors carefully state this is not proof of biology, it is "consistent with" biological methanogenesis producing excess CH₄ above the abiotic baseline — and the paper explicitly calculated that methanogenesis at rates comparable to Earth deep-sea hydrothermal systems would explain the discrepancy. Option B is incorrect: photolysis of wall-ice cannot produce H₂ at 0.9% volume — photolytic yields are far too low at Enceladus\'s distance from the Sun, and the D/H ratio in the H₂ was not cited in this way by Waite et al.; serpentinisation and photolysis produce H₂ with different isotopic signatures, and the Cassini data are consistent with hydrothermal (serpentinisation) origin. Option C is incorrect: primordial nebular H₂ would have escaped from Enceladus early in its history (Enceladus\'s escape velocity is only ~239 m/s; H₂ at typical thermal velocities would escape rapidly); noble-gas ratios consistent with solar nebula composition were not the finding of Waite et al. (2017). Option D is incorrect: radiolysis by Saturn\'s radiation belts is a real process, but the radiation dose at Enceladus\'s orbital distance (~3.95 Saturn radii) is far lower than at Io\'s orbit around Jupiter; quantitative radiolytic H₂ production at Enceladus\'s ice shell thickness is orders of magnitude too small to account for the observed 0.9% plume H₂; and the Waite et al. (2017) paper\'s findings were not correlated with magnetospheric activity variations.',
