          correct: 0,
          explain: 'Option A correctly identifies the physical principles, though the problem is richer than a single equation captures. The surface pressure equation P = M_atm × g / (4πR²) — where M_atm is the total atmospheric mass, g is surface gravity, and R is the body\'s radius — shows that a high surface pressure at low g requires a large M_atm / R² (a large atmospheric mass relative to the planet\'s surface area). Titan achieves this by a combination of factors: (1) Cold atmosphere, small scale height: the atmospheric scale height H = kT / (mg) (where k is Boltzmann\'s constant, T is temperature, m is molecular mass, g is gravitational acceleration) determines how rapidly atmospheric pressure decreases with altitude. Titan\'s exospheric temperature is very cold (~150 K at the homopause), which makes H small, concentrating atmospheric mass near the surface and maintaining a high surface pressure even with a modest total atmospheric inventory. Earth\'s scale height is ~8.5 km at the surface; Titan\'s is ~18 km in the troposphere but the atmosphere is much more vertically extended in mass because the low gravity and cold temperature allow a thick neutral gas layer to exist at altitudes where Earth\'s atmosphere is already negligible. (2) Non-condensing N₂: on Earth, water vapour (which can constitute up to ~4% of the atmosphere by volume in the tropics) condenses at altitude, effectively removing mass from the column. Titan\'s tropospheric gas is almost entirely N₂, which does not condense anywhere in Titan\'s atmosphere — it remains as a permanent inventory. This contrasts with a body like Venus, where CO₂ is also non-condensing. (3) UV shielding by tholins: the tholin haze layer absorbs UV photons that would otherwise photodissociate N₂ (producing N atoms that can escape) and CH₄. This partial photodissociation shield extends the effective lifetime of N₂ in the atmosphere. (4) Temperature and molecular weight: the thermal escape (Jeans escape) rate for N₂ from Titan is extremely slow because the Jeans parameter (λ = GMm/kTR) is large — N₂ is heavy enough that even at Titan\'s low gravity, only the high-energy tail of the Maxwellian velocity distribution can escape; the estimated N₂ Jeans escape flux is negligible compared to the atmospheric inventory. Option B is incorrect factually: N₂\'s molecular weight (28) is nearly identical to Earth\'s effective atmospheric molecular weight (~28.97), so molecular weight differences do not explain the pressure difference; furthermore, the Huygens probe did measure 1.467 bar (1.5 bar rounded), which is confirmed, not an overestimate. Option C is incorrect: tidal compression from Saturn on Titan\'s atmosphere is an extremely small effect — the tidal acceleration at Titan\'s surface is ~0.00012 m/s² (compared to Titan\'s own surface gravity of ~1.35 m/s²), orders of magnitude too small to account for a 50% difference in atmospheric pressure; moreover, tidally-locked bodies have permanent tidal bulges in their solid rock, not in their atmospheres, which rapidly equilibrate. Option D is speculative and without observational support: no evidence of a recent massive cryovolcanic outgassing event exists in the geological record of Titan\'s surface, and Jeans escape of N₂ on a ~billion-year timescale would have been mentioned in the relevant literature if plausible.',
