          correct: 0,
          explain: 'Option A correctly identifies tidal heating via the 2:1 resonance with Dione as the overwhelmingly dominant energy source. The argument proceeds in three steps. First, quantifying what primordial and radiogenic heat can contribute: for a body of Enceladus\'s size (~504 km diameter, mass ~1.08 × 10²⁰ kg) and composition (roughly 60% rock, 40% ice, with chondritic rock abundance of radioactive nuclides), total radiogenic heat production today is estimated at approximately 0.3 GW (primarily from ⁴⁰K, ²³²Th, ²³⁵U, ²³⁸U). Primordial heat from accretion would have been dissipated long ago given the small size and high surface-area-to-volume ratio of Enceladus. Together, radiogenic + primordial heat falls at least 50× short of the observed ~15.8 GW south polar heat output measured by Cassini CIRS. Second, identifying the tidal mechanism: Enceladus is in a 2:1 orbital mean-motion resonance with the larger moon Dione. Enceladus completes exactly two orbits for every one orbit of Dione. At each conjunction (when Enceladus and Dione are closest), Dione\'s gravitational kick on Enceladus re-excites Enceladus\'s orbital eccentricity. If Enceladus were not in this resonance, tidal dissipation would circularise its orbit on a timescale of millions of years and shut off tidal heating entirely. The resonance prevents this, sustaining the eccentricity and hence the tidal heating continuously. This is the exact same mechanism — mean-motion resonance maintaining orbital eccentricity — that operates on Io through the Laplace resonance with Europa and Ganymede, just applied to a two-body resonance rather than a three-body one. Third, the geometric focusing at the south pole: the specific pattern of tidal stress in Enceladus\'s ice shell — computed from the combination of static (synchronous rotation) and dynamic (periodic tidal cycle) stresses — produces maximum shear stress at the south polar region, specifically along orientations consistent with the observed tiger stripe azimuths. This geometric focusing of tidal stress concentrates both the heating and the fracturing at the south pole, explaining why the activity is localised there rather than distributed globally. Option B (impact heating) is inconsistent with the timescales: the thermal diffusivity of ice is ~10⁻⁶ m²/s; heat deposited by an impact at the surface of a ~250 km radius body would diffuse throughout the interior and escape to space in a timescale of R²/κ ~ (2.5 × 10⁵)² / (10⁻⁶) ~ 6 × 10¹⁶ s ~ 2 × 10⁹ years — a geologically plausible retention timescale, but the sustained heat output of ~15.8 GW implies continuous energy input, not gradual decay; moreover, an impact large enough to melt a significant interior fraction of Enceladus would itself be a highly improbable event. Option C (anomalous ⁴⁰K abundance) is not supported by cosmochemical constraints: Enceladus\'s bulk density and spectral properties are consistent with normal chondritic rock and water ice composition; ⁴⁰K at 10× solar abundance would require a completely exotic formation environment not indicated by any data. Option D (ring-particle infall) is energetically negligible: Saturn\'s ring particle infall rate and impact velocities onto Enceladus are far too small to generate GW-scale heating; the ring-infall energy is orders of magnitude below the observed thermal output.',
